s8-3-p24 = prose (cons "For a fixed labeled vertex type, put \(\mathsf{Matrix}_n:=\mathsf{Fin}(n)\to\mathsf{Fin}(n)\to\mathsf{Bool}\) and define the **type of canonical adjacency lists**, not merely the type of arbitrary lists, by" (cons "\[" (cons "\mathsf{AdjList}_n:=\sum_{L:\mathsf{Fin}(n)\to\mathsf{List}(\mathsf{Fin}(n))}" (cons "\prod_{i:\mathsf{Fin}(n)}" (cons "  (\mathsf{Sorted}(L(i))\times\mathsf{NoDup}(L(i)))." (cons "\]" (cons "Here \(\mathsf{Sorted}\) uses the standard decidable order on \(\mathsf{Fin}(n)\), and the displayed law components are propositions. Row membership gives \(\mathsf{AdjList}_n\to\mathsf{Matrix}_n\); enumerating each matrix row's true entries in increasing order gives the inverse. The matrix-to-list-to-matrix composite is pointwise identical by Boolean extensionality. The list-to-matrix-to-list composite is identical **because** each row is already sorted and duplicate-free, with the law witnesses identified by propositionality. Thus the equivalence is between \(\mathsf{Matrix}_n\) and \(\mathsf{AdjList}_n\), not between matrices and *all* lists. Without the canonicality laws, the latter composite yields a canonical representative rather than identity of arbitrary lists. Algorithms and correctness proofs are transported across the structured equivalence; graph relabeling and matrix/list conversion are distinct transformations." nil)))))))
